<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-5-3-ftc" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>
  <!-- Stewart 5.3 . Sessions 9-10 . Tue Sep 22 and Thu Sep 24 . Quiz 3 on Sep 24 (Skills 10-11)
       Built from Handout 7 ("Why the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Works") in the
       conversational handout voice. Written 2026-10-12.
       NOTATION: A(x) = int_a^x f(t) dt is a definite integral with a variable upper
       bound; this is legal under the sec-5-2 integral-sign unlock. Indefinite-integral
       notation (int f(x) dx with +C) does NOT appear here; that is quarantined to
       sec-5-4-indefinite-net-change. Antiderivatives keep being written as F with F'=f.
       Keeps reusing the paint-roller walls from sec-5-1/the Doenet FTC activity
       (f=3, A=3x and f=2x, A=x^2) and the car (v(t)=3t^2, answer 8) from sec-4-9/sec-5-2. -->

  <introduction>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-question-ftc">
      <title>Today's Question</title>
      <p>
        Two different jobs &#8212; <em>differentiating</em> and
        <em>accumulating</em> &#8212; keep landing on the same answers. Is that a
        coincidence?
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Pancake has been rolling paint onto walls since <xref ref="sec-5-1-areas-distances"/>,
      and measuring the area <m>\int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m> since
      <xref ref="sec-5-2-definite-integral"/>. Today she notices something her coach
      cannot ignore: every time she works out how <em>fast</em> the painted area
      is growing, she lands right back on the roller's speed <m>f</m>. That is
      either an enormous coincidence, or it is a theorem.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-conjecture">
    <title>Pancake's conjecture</title>

    <p>
      Remember the two walls. On Wall 1, the roller moves at a constant speed
      <m>f(x) = 3</m>, and the painted area after <m>x</m> feet is
      <m>A(x) = 3x</m>. On Wall 2, the roller speeds up as it goes,
      <m>f(x) = 2x</m>, and the painted area is <m>A(x) = x^2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now differentiate each area function.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-wall-derivatives">
      <title>Differentiate the area functions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For Wall 1, <m>A(x) = 3x</m>, so <m>A'(x) =</m>
          <fillin answer="3" mode="number"/>. For Wall 2, <m>A(x) = x^2</m>, so
          <m>A'(x) = 2x</m>, and at <m>x = 4</m> that is
          <fillin answer="8" mode="number"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes &#8212; <m>A'(x) = 3</m>, exactly the roller speed <m>f(x) = 3</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes &#8212; <m>A'(4) = 2(4) = 8</m>, exactly the roller speed <m>f(4) = 2(4) = 8</m> at that instant.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Both times, differentiating the <em>accumulated area</em> handed back the
      <em>roller speed</em> we started with. Pancake's conjecture:
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-conjecture">
      <title>Pancake's conjecture</title>
      <p>
        If <m>A(x)</m> is the area painted up to position <m>x</m>, then
        <m>A'(x) = f(x)</m>. Accumulating and then differentiating undoes
        itself.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Two examples are not a proof. We need to see <em>why</em> this has to be
      true for any reasonable <m>f</m>, not just these two.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-tiny-strip">
    <title>Look at one tiny piece of new area</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>A(x)</m> be the area accumulated from <m>a</m> up to <m>x</m>, for
      some fixed starting point <m>a</m>. Now step forward by a small amount
      <m>\Delta x</m>. The new area <m>A(x + \Delta x)</m> is the old area
      <m>A(x)</m> plus one thin strip: a sliver running from <m>x</m> to
      <m>x + \Delta x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      When <m>\Delta x</m> is small, that sliver is almost a rectangle: its
      width is <m>\Delta x</m>, and its height is approximately <m>f(x)</m>,
      the value of the roller speed right at the left edge of the strip. So
      <md>
        A(x + \Delta x) - A(x) \;\approx\; f(x)\,\Delta x.
      </md>
      This is exactly the running-triangle-plus-trapezoid-strip picture from
      the FTC paint-roller activity: the new paint added in one step is close
      to (rate) <m>\times</m> (step size), and the approximation gets better
      and better as the step shrinks.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-rate">
    <title>Turn the area change into a rate</title>

    <p>
      Divide both sides of <m>A(x+\Delta x) - A(x) \approx f(x)\,\Delta x</m> by
      <m>\Delta x</m>:
      <md>
        \frac{A(x+\Delta x) - A(x)}{\Delta x} \;\approx\; f(x).
      </md>
      That left-hand side should look familiar &#8212; it is exactly the
      difference quotient from the definition of the derivative. Let
      <m>\Delta x \to 0</m>:
      <md>
        A'(x) \;=\; \lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{A(x+\Delta x) - A(x)}{\Delta x} \;=\; f(x).
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This is exactly the slider demonstration from the Doenet pre-assignment:
      on Wall 1 the rate <m>\Delta A/\Delta x</m> sat still at <m>3</m> no
      matter the step size, while on Wall 2 the rate only settled on
      <m>f(x) = 2x</m> once <m>\Delta x</m> was squeezed down toward <m>0</m>.
      Squeezing <m>\Delta x \to 0</m> is precisely what turns an approximation
      into an exact derivative.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-ftc-part-1-informal">
      <title>Pancake's conjecture, proved</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A(x)</m> is the accumulated area up to <m>x</m>, then
          <m>A'(x) = f(x)</m>. This is no longer a guess &#8212; it follows from
          the definition of the derivative applied to one thin strip.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-notation">
    <title>Write the accumulation function using integral notation</title>

    <p>
      We already have a name for the accumulated area: it is a definite
      integral with a variable upper bound,
      <md>
        A(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\,dt.
      </md>
      (The variable inside is renamed <m>t</m> so it is not confused with the
      upper bound <m>x</m>.) Combining this with
      <xref ref="fact-ftc-part-1-informal"/> gives the first part of the
      Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-ftc-part-1">
      <title>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 1</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f</m> is continuous and <m>A(x) = \displaystyle\int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m>,
          then
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx}\left(\int_a^x f(t)\,dt\right) = f(x).
          </md>
          In words: differentiating an accumulation function gives back the
          rate you started with.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-fundamental">
    <title>What is so &#8220;fundamental&#8221; about it?</title>

    <p>
      Derivatives measure instantaneous <em>change</em>. Integrals measure
      total <em>accumulation</em>. These sound like opposite jobs &#8212; one
      zooms in, the other adds up. The Fundamental Theorem says they are
      secretly inverse operations: accumulate, then differentiate, and you
      land right back where you started. It is the calculus version of
      &#8220;undo a sum by subtracting.&#8221;
    </p>

    <p>
      There is a second consequence worth naming right away: since
      <m>A'(x) = f(x)</m>, the accumulation function <m>A</m> is itself <em>an
      antiderivative</em> of <m>f</m>. That one observation is about to unlock
      every definite integral we could not evaluate by geometry alone.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-examples-1">
    <title>Three accumulation functions</title>

    <p>
      <m>A(x) = \displaystyle\int_0^x 4\,dt</m>. A constant-height strip of
      height <m>4</m> out to <m>x</m> has area <m>A(x) = 4x</m>, so
      <m>A'(x) = 4</m> &#8212; exactly <m>f(x) = 4</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>A(x) = \displaystyle\int_0^x 2t\,dt</m>. This is the familiar triangle:
      <m>A(x) = x^2</m>, so <m>A'(x) = 2x</m> &#8212; exactly <m>f(x) = 2x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now a function with no obvious geometric shortcut:
      <m>A(x) = \displaystyle\int_1^x (t^2+3)\,dt</m>. Nothing about this region
      is a triangle or a rectangle, so geometry alone will not evaluate it.
      But FTC Part 1 does not care &#8212; it never asked us to compute the
      integral at all.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-ftc1-no-geometry" label="check-ftc1-no-geometry">
      <title>Differentiate without integrating</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A(x) = \displaystyle\int_1^x (t^2+3)\,dt</m>, then
          <m>A'(x) = x^2+3</m>. What is <m>A'(2)</m>?
          <fillin answer="7" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Yes, <m>A'(2) = 2^2+3 = 7</m>. We found the exact rate at which
                the painted area is growing at <m>x=2</m>, without ever
                computing the area itself.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-ftc-part-2">
    <title>From accumulation to a computation formula</title>

    <p>
      FTC Part 1 tells us <m>A</m> is <em>an</em> antiderivative of <m>f</m>.
      But in <xref ref="sec-4-9-antiderivatives"/> we learned that any two
      antiderivatives of the same <m>f</m> differ only by a constant. So if
      <m>F</m> is <em>any</em> antiderivative we already know how to find
      (power rule, trig rule, whatever), then
      <md>
        A(x) = F(x) + C
      </md>
      for some constant <m>C</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can pin down <m>C</m> using the one value of <m>A</m> we already know
      for free: <m>A(a) = \displaystyle\int_a^a f(t)\,dt = 0</m>, since
      accumulating from <m>a</m> to <m>a</m> accumulates nothing. So
      <m>0 = F(a) + C</m>, which means <m>C = -F(a)</m>. Substituting back,
      <md>
        \int_a^x f(t)\,dt = A(x) = F(x) - F(a).
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Nothing stops us from letting the ending point be any value <m>b</m>
      instead of a generic <m>x</m>. That gives the computational form of the
      theorem.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-ftc-part-2">
      <title>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>F</m> is any antiderivative of a continuous function <m>f</m>
          (that is, <m>F' = f</m>), then
          <md>
            \int_a^b f(x)\,dx = F(b) - F(a).
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      This one formula is the reason we spent all of
      <xref ref="sec-4-9-antiderivatives"/> building up antiderivative rules:
      every one of those rules now evaluates a definite integral instantly,
      with no limits and no rectangles.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-evaluation-bar">
    <title>The evaluation bar</title>

    <p>
      Writing <m>F(b) - F(a)</m> over and over gets repetitive, so we abbreviate
      it with an evaluation bar:
      <md>
        \bigl[F(x)\bigr]_a^b \;:=\; F(b) - F(a).
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Worked example: evaluate <m>\displaystyle\int_1^3 x^2\,dx</m>. An
      antiderivative of <m>x^2</m> is <m>F(x) = \tfrac13x^3</m>, so
      <md>
        \int_1^3 x^2\,dx = \left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_1^3 = \frac{27}{3} - \frac13 = 9 - \frac13 = \frac{26}{3}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="try-ftc-computation" label="try-ftc-computation">
      <title>You do: compute with FTC Part 2</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle\int_0^3 2x\,dx</m> using <m>F(x) = x^2</m>:
                <fillin answer="9" mode="number"/>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi/2} \cos x\,dx</m> using
                <m>F(x) = \sin x</m>: <fillin answer="1" mode="number"/>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes: <m>[x^2]_0^3 = 9 - 0 = 9</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes: <m>[\sin x]_0^{\pi/2} = 1 - 0 = 1</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-car">
    <title>Return to the car</title>

    <p>
      Back in <xref ref="sec-4-9-antiderivatives"/>, the car with velocity
      <m>v(t) = 3t^2</m> gave displacement <m>F(2) - F(0) = 8 - 0 = 8</m> using
      the antiderivative <m>F(t) = t^3</m>. Back in
      <xref ref="sec-5-2-definite-integral"/>, the same car gave
      <m>\int_0^2 3t^2\,dt = 8</m> as the limit of Riemann sums squeezing
      tighter and tighter. Two completely different procedures &#8212; one
      algebraic, one about rectangles &#8212; landed on the exact same number.
    </p>

    <p>
      That was never a coincidence. FTC Part 2 is <em>why</em> it had to come
      out the same way both times:
      <md>
        \int_0^2 3t^2\,dt = \bigl[t^3\bigr]_0^2 = 8 - 0 = 8.
      </md>
      The antiderivative method is not a different answer to a different
      question. It is a shortcut for computing the exact Riemann-sum limit,
      guaranteed by the Fundamental Theorem.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-no-plus-c">
    <title>What happened to the <m>+C</m>?</title>

    <p>
      You might expect <m>F(b) - F(a)</m> to carry a leftover constant. It
      does not, and it is worth seeing exactly why.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we used <m>F(x) + C</m> instead of <m>F(x)</m> as our antiderivative,
      <md>
        \bigl(F(b)+C\bigr) - \bigl(F(a)+C\bigr) = F(b) - F(a).
      </md>
      The constant appears in both terms and cancels exactly. So it never
      matters which antiderivative you pick &#8212; and there is never any need
      to write <m>+C</m> when evaluating a definite integral.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-no-plus-c">
      <title>Spot the error</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A student writes
          <m>\displaystyle\int_0^2 3x^2\,dx = x^3+C</m>. What went wrong?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              A definite integral is a <em>number</em>, not a family of
              functions &#8212; the bounds must be used. The correct computation
              is <m>[x^3]_0^2 = 8 - 0 = 8</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. No bounds were ever plugged in, and <m>+C</m> never belongs on a definite integral.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>Nothing &#8212; <m>x^3+C</m> is a correct antiderivative of <m>3x^2</m>.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback><p>It is a correct antiderivative, but the question asked for a definite integral &#8212; a single number found by evaluating at the bounds, not a family of functions.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-3-practice">
    <title>Putting it together</title>

    <exercise xml:id="check-ftc-conceptual">
      <title>Conceptual check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>In your own words, what does FTC Part 1 say about the relationship between accumulating and differentiating?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why is the accumulation function <m>A(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m> itself an antiderivative of <m>f</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does <m>+C</m> never appear in the final answer to a definite integral?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Differentiating an accumulation function gives back the exact rate that was being accumulated: <m>A'(x) = f(x)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Because FTC Part 1 says <m>A'(x) = f(x)</m>, which is exactly the defining property of an antiderivative.</p></li>
            <li><p>Because the constant appears in both <m>F(b)+C</m> and <m>F(a)+C</m>, and subtracting cancels it.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exit-ticket-ftc-1">
      <title>Exit ticket, part 1</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A(x) = \displaystyle\int_1^x (t^3+2)\,dt</m>, then
          <m>A'(x) =</m> ?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>x^3+2</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes &#8212; by FTC Part 1, differentiating the accumulation function just hands back the integrand.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p><m>\tfrac14 x^4+2x</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>That is an antiderivative of <m>t^3+2</m>, which is a different question from <m>A'(x)</m>. FTC Part 1 says <m>A'(x)</m> is just the integrand itself.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p><m>3x^2</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Not quite &#8212; that would be the derivative of <m>x^3</m> alone, but the integrand here is <m>t^3+2</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exit-ticket-ftc-2">
      <title>Exit ticket, part 2</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\int_0^2 3x^2\,dx =</m>
          <fillin answer="8" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes, <m>[x^3]_0^2 = 8-0 = 8</m>. Our car's number, one more time.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Today's main idea</title>
      <p>
        The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus ties the two halves of this
        chapter together:
        <md>
          \frac{d}{dx}\left(\int_a^x f(t)\,dt\right) = f(x)
          \qquad\text{and}\qquad
          \int_a^b f(x)\,dx = F(b) - F(a) \text{ when } F' = f.
        </md>
        Differentiating undoes accumulating. And once we know any single
        antiderivative, every definite integral becomes a two-step
        subtraction &#8212; no limits required.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next question: when there are <em>no</em> bounds at all, what does
        <m>\int f(x)\,dx</m> even mean, and how is it different from
        everything we just did? That is
        <xref ref="sec-5-4-indefinite-net-change" text="custom">Indefinite Integrals and the Net Change Theorem</xref>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>
</section>
